Q: How to add a static method to the built-in URL class? A: <p>The problem is quite solvable. I want to warn from the very beginning, that is has little to know practical sense, because you want to complement already existing constructor with a static function doing the same. But this is possible.</p>
<p>Basically, you need pure JavaScript. With TypeScript, there is one complication: you have to declare an interface, otherwise your new method <code>create</code> won't be recognized.</p>
<p>Now, let's consider this:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>A static function is the function property of a constructor function (or a class), not the property of the instance of the class.</p>
</li>
<li><p>A static function does not accept the argument <code>this</code>. So, this is the same as an <em>arrow function</em>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Having all that, let's implement <code>URL.create</code>:</p>
<pre><code>declare global { // not for JavaScript, only for TypeScript
  interface URLConstructor {
    create(url: string | URL, base?: string | URL): URL;
  }
}

// pure JavaScript: adding an arrow function property:
URL.create = (url, base) =&gt; new URL(url, base);

// now you can test it:

URL.create = (base, url) =&gt; new URL(base, url);
const baseUrl = new URL(&quot;https://stackoverflow.com&quot;);
const myUrl = new URL(&quot;questions&quot;, baseUrl);
console.log(myUrl);
// this is now recognized by TypeScript:
const newUrl = URL.create(&quot;questions&quot;, baseUrl);
console.log(myUrl);
console.log(newUrl); // same thing
</code></pre>
<p>Also note that the JavaScript class is just a <em>syntactic sugar</em> on top of the JavaScript <em>constructor functions</em>. So, an object like <code>URL</code> (don't confused this constructor function (or class) object with an URL instance) does not have to be a class. It can be just a function. Yes, you can add a new property to a function as well:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// let's give it a different name, UniformResourceLocator:
function UniformResourceLocator(url, base) {
   this.base = base;
   // and so long...
}

Adding a property to a function:
UniformResourceLocator.create = (url, base) =&gt;
    new UniformResourceLocator(url, base);
</code></pre>
<p>That's it.</p>

Q: How to convert UTF-8 URDU text to image on the fly? A: <p><strong>The Best solution</strong> and I close the quest</p>
<p><strong>Use PHP, HTML5 Canvas, and JS to convert UTF- Urdu text to image</strong></p>
<p>My PHP code  calls a simple function for  re-using  many times in same page</p>
<pre><code>echo generateCanvas($newCanvas, 'second', $textColor, $second , '2px', '8px'  , $cWIDTH );
</code></pre>
<p>And Function I made</p>
<pre><code>function generateCanvas($myCanvas, $className, $textColor , $misra , $cLEFT,  $cTOP ,$cWIDTH ) {
  $output = &quot;&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;&lt;span class=\&quot;$className\&quot;&gt;\n&quot;;  
  $output .= &quot;&lt;canvas id=\&quot;$myCanvas\&quot;  style='position: relative;   left: $cLEFT; top: $cTOP;  '  width='$cWIDTH' height='65'&gt; &quot;;
  $output .= &quot;&lt;/canvas&gt;\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;&lt;script&gt;\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;var canvas = document.getElementById('$myCanvas');\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;canvas.style.backgroundColor = 'yellow';\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;var cty = canvas.getContext('2d');\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;cty.font = '28px Jameel Noori Nastaleeq';\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;cty.fillStyle = '$textColor';\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;cty.fillText('$misra', 530, 50);\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;&lt;/script&gt;\n&quot;;
  $output .= &quot;&lt;/span&gt;\n&quot;;
  return $output;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I also added in my CSS file code to call font from my website,  for local host, the code to add these lines</p>
<pre><code>@font-face {
   font-family: 'Jameel Noori Nastaleeq';
  src: url('http://localhost/fonts/Jameel%20Noori%20Nastaleeq.ttf') format('truetype');

}
</code></pre>
