Q: How to read filenames saved as UTF-8 (urdu / arabic characters)? A: <p>I found the solution for Urdu.</p>
<p>I needed this line in code:</p>
<pre><code>
$file_utf8 = iconv( &quot;Windows-1256&quot;, &quot;utf-8&quot;, $sub );
</code></pre>
<p>I am posting the complete solution for helping others:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php 
$dir = &quot;.&quot;; 

function ReadFolderDirectory($dir) 
    { 
    $listDir = array(); 
    if($handler = opendir($dir)) { 
    while (($sub = readdir($handler)) !== FALSE) 
            { 
            if ($sub != &quot;.&quot; &amp;&amp; $sub != &quot;..&quot; &amp;&amp; $sub != &quot;Thumb.db&quot;) 
                { 
                if(is_file($dir.&quot;/&quot;.$sub)) 
                    { 
                    $file_utf8 = iconv( &quot;Windows-1256&quot;, &quot;utf-8&quot;, $sub );
                    $listDir[] = $sub; 
                    //  echo  $file_utf8 .&quot;&lt;br&gt;&quot;;
                    echo  $dir.&quot;/&quot;.$file_utf8 .&quot;&lt;br&gt;&quot;;
                    }
                elseif(is_dir($dir.&quot;/&quot;.$sub))
                    {
                    echo &quot;  New Sub Directory :  &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;     &quot;. $dir.&quot;/&quot;.$sub . &quot; &lt;br&gt; &quot;; 
                    $listDir[$sub] = ReadFolderDirectory($dir.&quot;/&quot;.$sub); 
                    } 
                } 
            }    
            closedir($handler); 
        } 

        return $listDir;    
    } 
  ReadFolderDirectory($dir);   
?&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: HTML5 equivalent to &lt;font&gt;? A: <p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/font#size" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/font#size</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>size (Deprecated)</p>
<p>This attribute specifies the font size as either a numeric or relative value. Numeric values range from 1 to 7 with 1 being the smallest and 3 the default. It can be defined using a relative value, like +2 or -3, which sets it relative to 3, the default value.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/absolute-size" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/absolute-size</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>&lt;absolute-size&gt;</p>
<p>The &lt;absolute-size&gt; CSS data type describes the absolute size keywords. This data type is used in the font shorthand and font-size properties.</p>
<p>The font size keywords are mapped to the deprecated HTML size attribute. See the HTML size attribute section below).</p>
</blockquote>
<div class="s-table-container"><table class="s-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>font size 1-7</th>
<th>css value</th>
<th>% medium</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>xx-small</td>
<td>60%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>small</td>
<td>89%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>medium</td>
<td>100%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>large</td>
<td>120%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>x-large</td>
<td>150%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>xx-large</td>
<td>200%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>xxx-large</td>
<td>300%</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>So the range 1-7 is actually mapped to css values according to the tables cited above, but yet those values translates to an actual font size relative to the <code>medium</code> value that yet depends on other factors like: the browser's built in default; the user's browser settings; and the <code>font-size</code> set on the html/body element.</p>
<p>In your position I would define 7 different css rules:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.font-size-1{ font-size: xx-small; }
.font-size-2{ font-size: small; }
.font-size-3{ font-size: medium; }
.font-size-4{ font-size: large; }
.font-size-5{ font-size: x-large; }
.font-size-6{ font-size: xx-large; }
.font-size-7{ font-size: xxx-large; }
</code></pre>
<p>Then I would calibrate the base <code>font-size</code> controlling <code>medium</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>html, body{ font-size: 16px; }
</code></pre>
<p>And I would replace all the font elements <code>&lt;font size=&quot;1-7&quot;&gt;content&lt;/font&gt;</code> using <code>&lt;span class=&quot;font-size-*&quot;&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;</code> where <code>*</code> is the font size mapped to the corresponding  css class.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
